      "content": "That \"Market Adjustment Factor\" is a clever way to handle the volatility, but I'm concerned about the audit trail. If an auditor looks at that formula, they're going to ask why we're using a proprietary multiplier to essentially nullify real-world market declines. \n\nThey might argue that a negative growth rate should, by definition, reduce the present value. To protect the bond application, I need you to substantiate this MAF. Can you provide the empirical or historical basis for using this specific formula? I need to be able to explain to the council why this multiplier is a statistically sound method for capturing \"lagged structural value\" rather than just an arbitrary adjustment to inflate the number.",
